The '''Jade Sea War''' (c. 1300 – c. 1390), known in the [[Kingdom of Zong]] as the '''War of the Eastern Defence''', was a prolonged conflict between the '''[[Chengzhi Dynasty]]''' of the '''[[Kingdom of Daras]]''' and the '''[[Kingdom of Zong]]'''. Fought over approximately ninety years in and around the '''[[Jade Sea]]''', the war arose from the Chengzhi court's invasion of '''[[Bingzhou]]''', one of the [[Three Luoyan States]] that was in personal union with Zong.

Zong successfully repelled the Darasian advance, preserving its territorial integrity and that of its dependencies. The strategic aftermath, however, favoured Daras: denied military conquest, the Chengzhi dynasty and its successors imposed a trade blockade that excluded Zong and the eastern seaboard from the overland and maritime routes to [[Illypnia]]. This blockade redirected the maritime energies of the eastern states across the [[Shendan Ocean]], leading to the Fosian colonisation of the [[New World]].

The war is conventionally divided into four phases: the '''Taizhu Offensive''' (c. 1300–1325), the '''Jade Sea Campaigns''' (c. 1325–1355), the '''Long Stalemate''' (c. 1355–1380), and the '''Heping Settlement''' (c. 1380–1390).

== Background ==

=== Darasian expansion ===

The '''[[Kingdom of Daras]]''' was a continental power of long standing. Successive dynasties had expanded the kingdom from its core in the interior plains outward in all directions. By the mid-13th century, Darasian armies had conquered the territories to the north and west — the regions corresponding to modern [[Prathes]] and [[Zanpakia]] — absorbing a patchwork of smaller states that lacked the organisation to resist sustained imperial pressure.

The subjugation of the '''[[Balausia Peninsula]]''' — the large northern peninsula separating the [[Jade Sea]] from the [[Kendriyan Ocean]] — was among the final achievements of this westward and northward expansion. The peninsula's conquest gave Daras its first direct access to the Jade Sea, through ports on the southwestern coast.

=== The Chengzhi Dynasty ===

The '''[[Chengzhi Dynasty]]''', which came to power in the late 13th century, inherited an empire that had largely exhausted the possibilities for expansion to the north and west. The dynasty's attention turned eastward, toward the seaboard — the most productive agricultural region on the continent, and the centre of Fosian maritime commerce.

=== The Kingdom of Zong ===

The '''[[Kingdom of Zong]]''' was the dominant power of the eastern Jade Sea. With a centralised monarchy, the largest fleet on the Jade Sea, and a tradition of maritime commerce extending across the [[Shendan Ocean]], Zong represented the primary obstacle to Darasian expansion eastward. Unlike the smaller polities of the region, Zong possessed the military and economic capacity to contest Darasian ambitions on equal terms.

Zong's strategic position was enhanced by a network of alliances and dependencies. The kingdom maintained diplomatic and commercial relationships with the [[The Jyunhap|Jyunhap]] federation to the north and the [[Three Luoyan States]] to the southwest. Most significantly, Zong was in personal union with '''[[Bingzhou]]''', the southwesternmost of the Three Luoyan States, which bordered Darasian territory.

=== The Three Luoyan States and the Jyunhap ===

The '''[[Three Luoyan States]]''' — Taiyuan (太原), Jinyang (晉陽), and Bingzhou (并州) — were a loose coalition of Jin Chinese-speaking polities in the southwestern Jade Peninsula. '''[[Bingzhou]]''', as the southwesternmost state, shared a border with Daras and was thus the most exposed to Darasian pressure. Zong's personal union with Bingzhou, established in the late 13th century, extended Zongese protection to the state and created the mechanism through which the wider war would be triggered.

To the north, the '''[[The Jyunhap|Jyunhap]]''' (聯合, "Union") was a federation of approximately thirty Cantonese-speaking kingdoms, dukedoms, and city-states that had consolidated in response to the Darasian threat. While not militarily powerful enough to resist Daras independently, the Jyunhap provided material and diplomatic support to Zong throughout the war.

=== Casus belli: The invasion of Bingzhou ===

The immediate cause of the Jade Sea War was the Darasian invasion of '''[[Bingzhou]]''' in c. 1300. The Chengzhi court, having completed the conquest of the Balausia Peninsula, sought to extend Darasian sovereignty over the remaining independent states of the eastern seaboard. Bingzhou, as the smallest and most exposed of the Luoyan States, was identified as the weakest point in the eastern defences.

The Darasian calculation failed to account for Zong's personal union with Bingzhou. When Darasian forces crossed the border into Bingzhou territory, Zong was treaty-bound to defend its dependency. The Kingdom of Zong declared war on Daras, mobilising its fleet and army for the defence of Bingzhou and the broader eastern seaboard.

== Course of the war ==

=== Phase I: The Taizhu Offensive (c. 1300–1325) ===

The war opened with a coordinated Darasian advance on two fronts. '''[[Zhao Tianming]]''' led the imperial armies eastward through the mountain interior toward Bingzhou and the western approaches to Zongese territory, while the Darasian southern fleet, operating from ports on the [[Balausia Peninsula]], entered the [[Jade Sea]] from the southwest.

The overland campaigns achieved significant initial gains. Darasian forces penetrated the border mountains and occupied several strategic passes, threatening Bingzhou and the Zongese frontier provinces. Zongese forces, under the command of '''[[Go Siok-bin]]''', organised a coordinated defence, establishing fortified positions in the mountain corridors that slowed the Darasian advance.

At sea, the Darasian fleet entered the Jade Sea in force but encountered immediate resistance from the Zongese navy. The first naval engagements of the war were inconclusive — neither side possessed sufficient superiority to control the sea — but Go's defence prevented the Darasian fleet from establishing a forward anchorage on the eastern coast.

By 1325, the Taizhu Offensive had stalled. The Darasian army held positions in the border mountains but could not break through to the coastal plains. The death of [[Chengzhi Taizhu]] in 1318 and the accession of [[Chengzhi Wuzhao]] brought new leadership but no immediate change in strategic circumstances.

=== Phase II: The Jade Sea Campaigns (c. 1325–1355) ===

'''[[Chengzhi Wuzhao]]''', the "Warrior Emperor," committed the full resources of the Darasian empire to the conflict. The Darasian fleet was expanded through a massive programme of shipbuilding in the southern yards, and '''[[Chen Haijun]]''' was appointed admiral with orders to achieve decisive control of the Jade Sea.

The period from 1325 to 1355 saw the most intense naval warfare of the conflict. Chen Haijun launched a succession of fleet-scale offensives into the central Jade Sea, targeting Zongese supply lines and attempting to cut maritime connections between Zong's heartland and its frontier provinces. The Zongese fleet, now under the command of '''[[Tan Beng-huat]]''', met each offensive with a strategy of attrition: engaging the Darasian fleet in running battles that exploited Zongese advantages in seamanship and local knowledge.

The turning point of this phase was the Zongese seizure of '''[[Bihong]]''' and '''[[Hape]]''', two islands guarding the northeastern passages of the Jade Sea. Tan Beng-huat identified the islands — then under Darasian garrison — as the key to controlling the sea's exits to the [[Shendan Ocean]]. In a series of amphibious operations conducted in the 1340s, Zongese forces captured both islands, expelled the Darasian garrisons, and fortified them as permanent naval bases. The loss of Bihong and Hape severed the Darasian fleet's access to the northeastern Jade Sea.

By 1355, the Darasian fleet had been reduced to approximately half its peak strength. The death of Wuzhao in 1352 marked the end of the offensive phase.

=== Phase III: The Long Stalemate (c. 1355–1380) ===

'''[[Chengzhi Lingdi]]''' inherited a war that neither side could win. The Darasian army still held positions in the border mountains, and the Darasian fleet retained control of the southwestern Jade Sea, but the resources to mount another major offensive were unavailable.

On the Zongese side, '''[[Lim Tek-seng]]''' maintained the naval positions secured by Tan Beng-huat but lacked the strength to carry the war into Darasian waters. Zong held the central and northeastern Jade Sea; Daras held the southwest. The border mountains remained contested but static.

The stalemate imposed severe costs on both sides. The disruption of Jade Sea trade affected Zongese commerce, while the conscription demands of the Darasian army depopulated agricultural districts in the western provinces.

=== Phase IV: The Heping Settlement (c. 1380–1390) ===

The accession of '''[[Chengzhi Heping]]''' in 1378 signalled a shift in Darasian strategy. Heping recognised that the eastern campaign had failed and authorised negotiations with Zong.

The settlement, concluded through a series of agreements circa 1390, established that Daras would withdraw all territorial claims to Bingzhou and the eastern seaboard, recognise Zongese sovereignty within its established borders, and fix the border along the mountain ranges of the interior. Zong retained possession of Bihong and Hape.

== Supporting powers ==

Throughout the war, Zong received support from the '''[[The Jyunhap|Jyunhap]]''' and the '''[[Three Luoyan States]]''', though neither contributed military forces on the scale of the principal belligerents.

The Jyunhap provided financial subsidies to the Zongese war effort and permitted the use of its ports for Zongese naval operations. The federation's primary contribution was diplomatic: by maintaining a unified front with Zong, the Jyunhap prevented Daras from finding allies or neutral parties among the smaller states of the northern seaboard.

The Three Luoyan States, with Bingzhou under Zongese protection, contributed militia and local defence forces to the defence of the southwestern frontier. Taiyuan and Jinyang provided material support and served as secondary supply routes when Darasian naval activity disrupted maritime commerce.

== Consequences ==

=== Trade blockade ===

The military outcome was a Zongese victory, but the strategic aftermath favoured Daras. The [[Chengzhi Dynasty]] and its successors adopted an alternative strategy: the systematic exclusion of Zong and the eastern states from the trade routes connecting Fosia to [[Illypnia]].

Daras controlled the continental interior and, through coordinated tariff policies and the denial of transit rights, imposed a de facto blockade on Zongese access to western markets. Eastern goods could reach [[Illypnia]] only through Darasian intermediaries, on terms those intermediaries controlled.

=== The eastward turn ===

The trade blockade redirected Zong's maritime energies across the [[Shendan Ocean]]. Denied access to western markets, the Zongese merchant class and royal court sought new sources of wealth beyond the eastern horizon. Zongese navigators reached the western coast of the [[New World]] by the 1440s–1450s, establishing colonies including '''[[Tamsui]]'''.

== Legacy ==

The Jade Sea War established Zong as the dominant power of the eastern Jade Sea and demonstrated the capacity of a maritime state to resist continental imperial expansion. '''[[Tan Beng-huat]]''' is commemorated as the architect of Zongese naval supremacy. The war also created the trade blockade that, by closing western routes, prompted the Fosian colonisation of the New World from the east.

